<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Fermanagh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Fermanagh is a county in the south-west of Northern Ireland, centred on the lakes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-erne">Lough Erne</a> and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enniskillen">Enniskillen</a>. It takes its name from the Fir Manach, an early medieval population group, and from the 13th century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> it was ruled by the Maguire lords. It was made an English shire in 1585.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-fermanagh.jpg" alt="County Fermanagh" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lozleader · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferm_arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
